  "slug": "ou-khatta",
  "title": "Ou khatta",
  "odia": "ଓଉ ଖଟା",
  "lead": "Look closely at how an accompaniment is made, then follow it from the pan to its place in a meal.",
  "hero": "ou-khatta",
  "save_id": "food:ou-khatta",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "opening",
      "title": null,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "id": "opening",
          "text": "A dish name is a starting point, not a complete description. To understand a bowl of ou khatta, ask what happens before the fruit reaches the pan, what joins it there, and how it is served. Those questions let us compare choices without choosing a single version as the standard.",
          "source_ids": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "preparations",
      "title": "Two preparations, one cook",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "id": "sweet",
          "text": "In her 2017 sweet elephant-apple relish, Sasmita cooks and drains the fruit before moving it to another pan. Cumin and mustard seeds temper the oil; ginger, jaggery or sugar, and coconut follow. She also allows the cooked pieces to be crushed. Even within this recipe, texture is a choice.",
          "source_ids": [
            "food-ou-sasmita2017"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "paste",
          "text": "Her 2019 sweet-and-sour version uses a mustard–cumin paste alongside panch phoron in the oil. She blends the paste separately; the whole spices go into hot oil before the cooked fruit is added. The precooked, drained fruit then meets the paste and jaggery. She suggests rice and dalma alongside it. These are two recipes by the same cook, offering variation rather than evidence from two households.",
          "source_ids": [
            "food-ou-sasmita2019"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "comparison",
          "text": "Reading a method this way changes the comparison. An ingredient list tells us what is present; the sequence tells us what the cook does with it. When asking someone about their version, “What goes into the pan first?” can open a more useful conversation than “Which version is correct?”",
          "source_ids": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "meal",
      "title": "A place in the meal",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "id": "memory",
          "text": "Julie Acharya Ray’s 2014 memoir places ou khatta in her mother’s Kartik meal. On banana leaves, rice and dal occupy the centre, with vegetable dishes and ou khatta around them. This is one family’s remembered arrangement, not a menu for every household.",
          "source_ids": [
            "food-ou-ray2014"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "closing",
          "text": "The recipe and the remembered meal answer different questions. One follows the work of making an accompaniment; the other returns it to the food eaten beside it. Read them together, and there is more to ask about a small bowl than its name: how it was prepared, who prepared it, and what else was on the plate.",
          "source_ids": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
